7e-06,-1.4305104514278355e-06,-7.152555099310121e-07],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10048,2900,264,729,400,69,2075,15087,702,7947,400,4080,59,258,36958,26266,258,36958,15087,323,2088,400,7609,16,16,11,18,60,12947,1416,582,6979,264,501,729,400,70,2075,15087,553,26107,70,2075,8,284,282,7,21,87,7257,16,4779,3,1221,1128,374,279,2088,315,400,70,2075,15087,30,17399,697,4226,304,9873,44197,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nSuppose a function $f(x)$ has domain $(-\\infty,\\infty)$ and range $[-11,3]$. If we define a new function $g(x)$ by $$g(x) = f(6x)+1,$$ then what is the range of $g(x)$? Express your answer in interval notation.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"275ea3f846ed1c43016b2cf85c2c92cdc8316abae765eb7c0ce72d368cfb68fc"},"seconds":11.558732032775879,"seed":450667936848541482,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the range of the function \\( g(x) = f(6x) + 1 \\), we need to analyze how the transformation affects the range of the original function \\( f(x) \\).\n\n1. **Identify the range of \\( f(x) \\):**\n   The range of \\( f(x) \\) is given as \\([-11, 3]\\). This means that for any \\( x \\) in the domain of \\( f \\), the value of \\( f(x) \\) lies between \\(-11\\) and \\(3\\), inclusive.\n\n2. **Understand the transformation \\( g(x) = f(6x) + 1 \\):**\n   The function \\( g(x) \\) is defined as \\( f(6x) \\) shifted up by 1. This means that every value of \\( f(6x) \\) will be increased by 1 to obtain the corresponding value of \\( g(x) \\).\n\n3. **Determine the range of \\( f(6x) \\):**\n   Since \\( f(x) \\) has a range of \\([-11, 3]\\), the function \\( f(6x) \\) will also have the same range because the argument \\( 6x \\) is just a horizontal scaling of \\( x \\), which does not affect the range of the function. Therefore, the range of \\( f(6x) \\) is also \\([-11, 3]\\).\n\n4. **Shift the range of \\( f(6x) \\) by 1:**\n   To find the range of \\( g(x) = f(6x) + 1 \\), we need to add 1 to every value in the range of \\( f(6x) \\). So, we add 1 to the endpoints of the interval \\([-11, 3]\\):\n   \\[\n   -11 + 1 = -10 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 3 + 1 = 4.\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the range of \\( g(x) \\) is \\([-10, 4]\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{[-10, 4]}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{[-10, 4]}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:1052"}
